<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- The core: everything that computes and everything that formats. It knows nothing about the
       console or the windows, nor does it need to - that is why two applications could appear
       over it without a single figure being duplicated.

       The split is not cosmetic: before, "Analysis/ and Report/ are pure" was a convention kept
       by goodwill. Now it is a compile boundary - nothing here CAN reach Console, because there
       is no entry point to call it.

       The version and the copyright are NOT here: they come from Directory.Build.props, so that
       they are the same in all three assemblies. See the comment there. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The external dependencies. The rule is in docs/design/dependencies.md, "The rule": they
       are discussed beforehand and approved, neither avoided nor added silently. The licence of
       each and WHY it is compatible with GPLv3 are written in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md. What stays
       written by hand - FITS, PNG, all the computations - stays so because there it is honest.

       Sdcb.LibRaw, and only for camera raw files: there is NO purely managed .NET library that
       decodes the Bayer mosaic of a NEF. It binds the very libraw that rawpy uses in
       flatcheck.py too, so the figures come out bit-identical. Any alternative either
       debayers (and then the exposure mode has nothing to measure), or is commercial, or starts
       an external dcraw.exe. The runtime.* packages bring native DLLs (raw_r, jpeg8, lcms2,
       zlib1, ~2.1 MB); they go into flatcheck.exe ONLY at a publish with
       IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract (see README). Without them the FITS modes work
       unchanged; only the raw paths refuse, with an explicit message.

       SixLabors.ImageSharp, and only for the JPEG preview the camera embedded in the raw - the
       image from which it computed its on-screen histogram. It replaces nothing written by
       hand: the alternative was a JPEG decoder of our own, with Huffman, DCT and subsampling,
       for a picture that votes on nothing. Purely managed, so PublishSingleFile does not
       change. The version is PINNED for legal reasons too: the Six Labors licence is
       conditional and the grant comes with the version taken. Touched from a single file,
       PreviewReader.cs, so that it can be replaced if the licence moves.

       WHY 3.1.x AND NOT 4.x: since 4.0.0 the package brings an MSBuild task that asks for a
       licence key at compile time - on Debug a warning, on Release an ERROR. Open source
       projects get the key for free, but on request, and anyone who clones the repo would need
       their own to publish. 3.1.x has exactly the same licence terms (Split License 1.0, the
       Apache 2.0 branch for open source) and no task; the only API difference that concerns us
       is that 3.x never applies the ICC profile when decoding, which is exactly the behaviour
       wanted. See THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Sdcb.LibRaw" />
    <PackageReference Include="Sdcb.LibRaw.runtime.win64" />
    <PackageReference Include="Sdcb.LibRaw.runtime.linux64" />
    <PackageReference Include="SixLabors.ImageSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The core stays internal entirely. No public surface was built for it because it has no
       public: the four consumers (the console, the window and the two test projects) are in the
       same repo, compile together, and making the result records public would have meant
       turning their fields into properties only to please a compiler.

       The WPF application does not bind XAML directly to these records (WPF binding does not see
       fields, only properties) - it has view-models that read them and expose exactly what
       must be shown. So the list below is not a loophole, it is the contract. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="flatcheck" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="FlatCheck.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="FlatCheck.Wpf" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="FlatCheck.Wpf.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
